PATCH 552: forty-eight variables narrowed to the byte the image actually accesses.

This is the systematic pass of the class that patches 546, 547, 549 and 550 each hit one instance of.
A too-WIDE declaration is the damaging direction: the macro overlaps its neighbour, so every store
corrupts an unrelated live variable.  Patch 547 (the mission clock) and 550 (the victory counter) were
both found by chasing a symptom back to one of these; the point of this patch is to stop finding them
one corrupted subsystem at a time.

## How the set was chosen

`tools/width_triage.py --byte` (board:0023) scans every `DAT_2000_*` macro in the tree, locates each
one's 2-byte absolute displacement in `re_out/fist_dat_image.bin`, and classifies every reference by
the opcode immediately preceding it -- modrm /06 and moffs forms, with a 0x66 operand-size prefix
within the preceding five bytes marking 32-bit.  A macro enters this patch only if ALL FOUR hold:

  1. declared `undefined2`;
  2. at least one BYTE-width reference in the image;
  3. ZERO word-width and ZERO 32-bit references;
  4. ZERO unclassified references -- a 2-byte match whose preceding opcode is in neither table is
     either data or an addressing form the scanner does not know, and the one reference that would
     disprove the narrowing could be exactly that one.  128 macros pass 1-3; only these pass 4 as well.

and it has a LIVE SUCCESSOR: the next byte is itself referenced by the image, so the too-wide store was
actually corrupting something.  A too-wide byte with a dead neighbour is harmless and is left alone.

The classifier is validated against a patch whose answer is already known: patch 539 widened
`DAT_2000_5684` to a dword, and the scanner reports 11 of its 13 references as genuinely 32-bit.

Every use of all 48 was then audited for anything that depends on the wide read -- a comparison or
assignment against a constant above 0xff, a `-1` sentinel, an address-taken use, a shift.  Exactly ONE
came back, and it is handled below.  The other 47 are declaration-only changes.

## The one with width-dependent uses: DGROUP:0xe918

Six references, every one byte-width, sentinel 0xff:

    d243: c6 06 18 e9 ff    movb  $0xff,0xe918
    d264: 80 3e 18 e9 ff    cmpb  $0xff,0xe918
    d272: 80 3e 18 e9 02    cmpb  $0x2,0xe918
    d337: a2 18 e9          mov   %al,0xe918
    d3b8: c6 06 18 e9 02    movb  $0x2,0xe918
    d3c5: c6 06 18 e9 00    movb  $0x0,0xe918

Its `= -1` and two `!= -1` become 0xff.  They have to change here rather than in a sentinel patch: an
unsigned char promotes to int, so once narrowed the `-1` form would be a constant false (board:0022).

